from (values
('a2df767e-e24a-58c0-8472-84ba65275c3a'::uuid,'ZQ-0401','population-health-ethics','breast-screening','A 58-year-old woman attends a Melbourne general practice for a repeat prescription of transdermal oestradiol. She has no breast symptoms. Her mother is alive at 84, and an aunt developed breast cancer at 71. Her last BreastScreen mammogram, 26 months ago, was normal but reported heterogeneously dense breast tissue. She asks whether she should have MRI or ultrasound instead of another mammogram. Examination is normal.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Arrange her next BreastScreen mammogram now.","Replace mammography with annual screening MRI.","Replace mammography with screening ultrasound because of breast density.","Wait until age 65 before further screening.","Order CA 15-3 and image only if it is elevated."]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER
A. Arrange her next BreastScreen mammogram now.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
BreastScreen Australia offers biennial mammography to women aged 50–74, with access also available to eligible women aged 40–49 and those over 74. At 58, she is overdue for routine screening. Breast density can reduce mammographic sensitivity, but density alone does not make MRI or ultrasound the routine population screening replacement.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Correct. Her two-year screening interval has elapsed.
B. MRI is not the routine population screening test for an average-risk woman with this history.
C. Ultrasound is not a routine substitute for mammography based on density alone.
D. There is no reason to defer screening until 65.
E. CA 15-3 is not a population screening test for breast cancer.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply Australian breast screening eligibility and distinguish breast density from a high-risk surveillance indication.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply Australian breast screening eligibility and distinguish breast density from a high-risk surveillance indication.

EVIDENCE
BreastScreen Australia: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia','moderate','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0401","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-01","group_population_health_ethics","sys_breast_screening","task_management","level_3","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","screening_eligibility","interval_adherence","overinvestigation","breast_density","risk_stratification"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"BreastScreen Australia\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Correct. Her two-year screening interval has elapsed.","B":"MRI is not the routine population screening test for an average-risk woman with this history.","C":"Ultrasound is not a routine substitute for mammography based on density alone.","D":"There is no reason to defer screening until 65.","E":"CA 15-3 is not a population screening test for breast cancer."},"key_takeaways":["Apply Australian breast screening eligibility and distinguish breast density from a high-risk surveillance indication."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"BreastScreen Australia","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"f545dacd-842c-50fe-9e4c-d7c279342b8c","B":"f9dc6812-a083-5376-babd-323f5f85d692","C":"1ef0509c-a364-57e2-834c-aea9af619e61","D":"2bdac6f7-ffa2-570c-8d81-b03d1d4cff98","E":"ad4387b1-6230-517f-a027-d77541353703"},"correct_option_id":"f545dacd-842c-50fe-9e4c-d7c279342b8c","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-01","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,0,1.0,'tier=3 (ph01)'),
('10ac7eb8-4268-593f-9023-8df1279d4083'::uuid,'ZQ-0402','population-health-ethics','breast-screening','A 46-year-old woman in Adelaide asks her GP for a BreastScreen referral after a friend says free mammograms are only available from age 50. She has no breast symptoms. Her sister had a fibroadenoma removed at 29, with no family history of breast or ovarian cancer. Examination is normal.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["She must wait until age 50 before she can access BreastScreen.","She can access BreastScreen now, although routine invitations generally begin at 50.","She requires annual mammography because she requested screening before 50.","She should have annual screening ultrasound instead.","A normal examination means no imaging is needed until 50."]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER
B. She can access BreastScreen now, although routine invitations generally begin at 50.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Women aged 40–49 can access BreastScreen Australia, although the program routinely invites women aged 50–74. A fibroadenoma in a relative is not equivalent to a family history of breast cancer.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Incorrect. Access is not restricted to women aged 50 or older.
B. Correct. She can discuss and arrange screening now.
C. Requesting screening early does not automatically establish a need for annual imaging.
D. Ultrasound is not the standard population screening alternative.
E. Clinical examination cannot replace appropriate screening mammography.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Explain the difference between routine invitation age and program access.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Explain the difference between routine invitation age and program access.

EVIDENCE
BreastScreen Australia: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia','moderate','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0402","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-02","group_population_health_ethics","sys_breast_screening","task_management","level_3","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","screening_eligibility","risk_interpretation","patient_education","unnecessary_testing"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"BreastScreen Australia\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Incorrect. Access is not restricted to women aged 50 or older.","B":"Correct. She can discuss and arrange screening now.","C":"Requesting screening early does not automatically establish a need for annual imaging.","D":"Ultrasound is not the standard population screening alternative.","E":"Clinical examination cannot replace appropriate screening mammography."},"key_takeaways":["Explain the difference between routine invitation age and program access."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"BreastScreen Australia","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"62a47ab5-89c2-52ad-9dae-a4ebfd68eaf3","B":"4dbe3f58-d319-54f6-9fe7-ce324fc03e09","C":"6088fd84-0093-559a-a46a-467fc8e615f8","D":"65ae9f9d-8b12-573b-a07b-3ba508044335","E":"a1f71e87-828d-5664-8acf-b0facfcf3401"},"correct_option_id":"4dbe3f58-d319-54f6-9fe7-ce324fc03e09","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-02","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,0,1.0,'tier=3 (ph01)'),
('d783d19e-4aaf-52e9-a6b8-40a4f9b53586'::uuid,'ZQ-0403','population-health-ethics','breast-screening','A 44-year-old woman in Brisbane reports a firm lump in her left breast that has persisted for three weeks. She planned to wait until she reached screening age. Examination confirms a 2 cm firm, mobile mass without palpable axillary nodes. A BreastScreen appointment is available in 10 days, and the nurse suggests using it to avoid delay.

What is the most appropriate next step?','["Use the screening appointment and act only if the report is abnormal.","Reassure her that a mobile lump can be reviewed in six months.","Arrange prompt diagnostic assessment of the breast lump.","Start tamoxifen and repeat examination in three months.","Order CA 15-3 and a chest X-ray before deciding about imaging."]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER
C. Arrange prompt diagnostic assessment of the breast lump.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
A palpable breast mass requires diagnostic assessment, commonly involving clinical examination, age-appropriate diagnostic imaging and tissue sampling when indicated. A population screening appointment is not a substitute for investigating a symptom.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Screening pathways are designed for asymptomatic people and may not provide the necessary diagnostic work-up.
B. Mobility does not reliably exclude malignancy; delay is inappropriate.
C. Correct. Arrange prompt diagnostic assessment.
D. Tamoxifen should not be started empirically for an undiagnosed breast mass.
E. Tumour markers and chest X-ray are not the initial diagnostic pathway for an isolated breast lump.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Recognise when symptoms override routine screening eligibility.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Recognise when symptoms override routine screening eligibility.

EVIDENCE
Cancer Australia: https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/','difficult','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0403","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-03","group_population_health_ethics","sys_breast_screening","task_management","level_4","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","screening_vs_diagnosis","red_flag_recognition","diagnostic_delay","appropriate_referral"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Cancer Australia\",\"url\":\"https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Screening pathways are designed for asymptomatic people and may not provide the necessary diagnostic work-up.","B":"Mobility does not reliably exclude malignancy; delay is inappropriate.","C":"Correct. Arrange prompt diagnostic assessment.","D":"Tamoxifen should not be started empirically for an undiagnosed breast mass.","E":"Tumour markers and chest X-ray are not the initial diagnostic pathway for an isolated breast lump."},"key_takeaways":["Recognise when symptoms override routine screening eligibility."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Cancer Australia","url":"https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"b265d980-4eaf-58b1-b37f-a685b64d8572","B":"353cf076-9425-53f2-910d-db53b25a63a3","C":"3dcfb280-52e5-5c0a-957c-7f797117556c","D":"68e48e5f-5bbf-5b53-90e4-0c18e6637eb8","E":"ebd09973-d66a-5f60-b58a-7caa77f79119"},"correct_option_id":"3dcfb280-52e5-5c0a-957c-7f797117556c","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-03","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,1,1.0,'tier=4 (ph01)'),
('1920dcd8-1e6c-5cfa-afde-c89b0bb83288'::uuid,'ZQ-0404','population-health-ethics','breast-screening','A 76-year-old woman in Perth remains independent, drives and has well-controlled hypertension and osteoarthritis. She has attended BreastScreen every two years since age 52. Her last mammogram was normal 18 months ago. A friend tells her that breast screening is prohibited after age 74. She has no symptoms.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Arrange annual MRI because routine invitations have stopped.","Continue mandatory annual mammography until age 80.","Replace mammography with annual clinical examination.","Explain that routine invitations stop after 74, but she may discuss and choose further screening.","Explain that mammography is contraindicated after age 74."]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER
D. Explain that routine invitations stop after 74, but she may discuss and choose further screening.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Routine BreastScreen invitations target women aged 50–74. Women older than 74 may still access screening, but the potential benefits, harms, life expectancy, health status and preferences should inform the decision.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. MRI is not the automatic replacement for routine mammography.
B. Annual mammography is not mandatory.
C. Clinical examination alone is not an equivalent screening substitute.
D. Correct. Use shared decision-making about further screening.
E. Screening is not categorically contraindicated solely because of age.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply individualised decision-making when a patient is outside the routine screening invitation age.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply individualised decision-making when a patient is outside the routine screening invitation age.

EVIDENCE
BreastScreen Australia: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia','moderate','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0404","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-04","group_population_health_ethics","sys_breast_screening","task_management","level_3","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","age_based_eligibility","shared_decision_making","screening_benefit_harm","over_screening"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"BreastScreen Australia\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"MRI is not the automatic replacement for routine mammography.","B":"Annual mammography is not mandatory.","C":"Clinical examination alone is not an equivalent screening substitute.","D":"Correct. Use shared decision-making about further screening.","E":"Screening is not categorically contraindicated solely because of age."},"key_takeaways":["Apply individualised decision-making when a patient is outside the routine screening invitation age."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"BreastScreen Australia","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/breastscreen-australia","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"df2b4f2e-fb9c-5b6c-9fad-5b165026f731","B":"abc3047b-3c77-569b-aaa1-59ac3658dc75","C":"4534eb0f-ba6f-5498-8ff5-ff40b4e2bd55","D":"c70734ba-eddd-5ef4-a73f-57c7ea6353a2","E":"1d4b88d7-1941-5f6f-968f-3b437e377d23"},"correct_option_id":"c70734ba-eddd-5ef4-a73f-57c7ea6353a2","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-04","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,0,1.0,'tier=3 (ph01)'),
('dd79ca7a-b75d-56ab-9b53-d0c7a5a7c392'::uuid,'ZQ-0405','population-health-ethics','breast-screening','A 36-year-old woman in Sydney is eight weeks pregnant. Genetic testing has identified a pathogenic BRCA1 variant, and her mother developed breast cancer at 38. She has no breast symptoms. A midwife tells her that breast screening is unnecessary until population screening age and can be reconsidered after childbearing.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Wait until age 50 for routine BreastScreen invitations.","Use annual ultrasound as the sole long-term surveillance plan.","Arrange prophylactic bilateral mastectomy immediately, without further assessment.","Defer all risk management until she has completed childbearing.","Refer for specialist familial cancer and high-risk breast surveillance advice, taking pregnancy into account."]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER
E. Refer for specialist familial cancer and high-risk breast surveillance advice, taking pregnancy into account.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
A pathogenic BRCA1 variant and a strong early-onset family history warrant specialist risk management. The surveillance plan must be individualised and account for pregnancy, imaging choices, risk-reducing options and reproductive preferences.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Average-risk screening ages are not sufficient for this high-risk patient.
B. Ultrasound alone is not automatically an adequate lifelong surveillance plan.
C. Risk-reducing surgery requires specialist counselling and shared decision-making; it is not an automatic immediate intervention.
D. Risk assessment should not be postponed until childbearing is complete.
E. Correct. Refer to an appropriate familial cancer/high-risk service.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Distinguish population screening from surveillance for inherited cancer susceptibility.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Distinguish population screening from surveillance for inherited cancer susceptibility.

EVIDENCE
