('06c3f90c-b3b0-5001-83b2-9ce3e0f7dbea'::uuid,'ZQ-0406','population-health-ethics','breast-screening','A 62-year-old asymptomatic man in Hobart asks to join the same BreastScreen program as his wife. He has no breast symptoms or family history and is willing to pay privately if necessary. Examination is normal.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Routine population breast screening is not offered to asymptomatic men; assess any future symptoms promptly.","Enrol him in biennial mammography because he is older than 50.","Arrange annual breast ultrasound as the male equivalent of BreastScreen.","Measure CA 15-3 every two years.","Prescribe tamoxifen as a standard male breast cancer prevention program."]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER
A. Routine population breast screening is not offered to asymptomatic men; assess any future symptoms promptly.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
BreastScreen Australia is a population screening program for women. An asymptomatic man without a specific high-risk indication is not enrolled in an equivalent routine population program.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Correct. Explain the program scope and safety-net new symptoms.
B. Male sex and age alone do not create eligibility for routine BreastScreen.
C. Ultrasound is not a standard population screening substitute.
D. CA 15-3 is not a screening test.
E. Tamoxifen is not a routine prevention prescription for asymptomatic men.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Identify the intended population of a national screening program.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Identify the intended population of a national screening program.

EVIDENCE
