('c256477a-46dd-5137-9b43-c58cdb0af20a'::uuid,'ZQ-0407','population-health-ethics','bowel-screening','A 47-year-old man in Canberra has no bowel symptoms and no relevant family history. His wife, aged 52, has received a National Bowel Cancer Screening Program kit. He has never screened and asks whether he is too young because he remembers the program starting at 50.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["He must wait until age 50 before any program screening is possible.","He can request a free bowel screening kit now; routine automatic mail-outs begin at age 50.","He should undergo colonoscopy because his wife received a kit.","He should request a kit only if he develops rectal bleeding.","He should have annual CEA testing until age 50."]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER
B. He can request a free bowel screening kit now; routine automatic mail-outs begin at age 50.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Eligible Australians aged 45–49 can request their first free kit. People aged 50–74 are routinely mailed a kit every two years. Screening is intended for people without symptoms.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Incorrect. Eligibility begins at 45.
B. Correct. He can request his first kit.
C. His wife''s invitation does not indicate that he needs colonoscopy.
D. Screening should occur before symptoms arise; symptoms require diagnostic assessment instead.
E. CEA is not a population screening test.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply the current National Bowel Cancer Screening Program age rules.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply the current National Bowel Cancer Screening Program age rules.

EVIDENCE
