('d69ec148-52e0-5398-a9aa-115284ea09c2'::uuid,'ZQ-0408','population-health-ethics','bowel-screening','A 63-year-old woman in Darwin receives a bowel screening kit. She had haemorrhoids banded six years ago and has had no bleeding since. She has no weight loss, change in bowel habit or known anaemia. She asks whether she should discard the kit and arrange colonoscopy because a friend of similar age had one.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Arrange colonoscopy because everyone over 60 requires it instead of a kit.","Keep the kit until she notices bleeding.","Complete and return the kit if she remains asymptomatic and otherwise eligible.","Request CEA before deciding whether to screen.","Use the kit only if a blood test demonstrates anaemia."]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER
C. Complete and return the kit if she remains asymptomatic and otherwise eligible.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
An eligible, asymptomatic person aged 45–74 should participate in biennial screening. Remote, successfully treated haemorrhoids do not by themselves require colonoscopy instead of screening. New symptoms or a relevant personal or family history would warrant individual assessment.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Colonoscopy is not automatically required solely because of age.
B. Screening is intended before symptoms develop.
C. Correct. Complete the kit if no exclusion or alternative pathway applies.
D. CEA is not a screening test.
E. Screening does not require anaemia.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Distinguish routine screening from investigation prompted by symptoms or increased risk.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Distinguish routine screening from investigation prompted by symptoms or increased risk.

EVIDENCE
