('533cbdf8-0313-5046-b27d-97b742bfaee5'::uuid,'ZQ-0410','population-health-ethics','bowel-screening','A 60-year-old man in Geelong reports bright-red rectal bleeding on most days for three weeks. His haemoglobin is 132 g/L. Rectal examination shows no fissure or prolapsed haemorrhoid. He has a bowel screening kit due this month and asks to complete it before deciding whether to seek further care.

What is the most appropriate next step?','["Give him the kit and discharge him if the result is negative.","Repeat the kit after the bleeding has stopped for six weeks.","Order CEA and arrange colonoscopy only if it is elevated.","Reassure him that bright-red bleeding is usually haemorrhoidal.","Arrange prompt diagnostic assessment for the rectal bleeding rather than relying on screening."]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER
E. Arrange prompt diagnostic assessment for the rectal bleeding rather than relying on screening.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Rectal bleeding is a symptom requiring clinical assessment. The clinician should determine the urgency and appropriate investigation based on age, persistence, examination, blood results and other risk factors. A screening kit is not an adequate substitute.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. A negative screening test does not exclude significant disease in a symptomatic patient.
B. Waiting for symptoms to resolve may delay diagnosis.
C. CEA is not a diagnostic gatekeeper for colonoscopy.
D. Bright-red blood does not establish a benign cause.
E. Correct. Investigate the symptom through the appropriate diagnostic pathway.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Recognise that symptomatic patients should not be managed solely through population screening.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Recognise that symptomatic patients should not be managed solely through population screening.

EVIDENCE
Australian Government: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/doing-a-bowel-screening-test/who-should-do-the-bowel-screening-test-and-who-should-not','difficult','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0410","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-10","group_population_health_ethics","sys_bowel_screening","task_management","level_4","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","red_flag_recognition","symptom_based_investigation","diagnostic_delay","screening_misuse"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Australian Government\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/doing-a-bowel-screening-test/who-should-do-the-bowel-screening-test-and-who-should-not\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"A negative screening test does not exclude significant disease in a symptomatic patient.","B":"Waiting for symptoms to resolve may delay diagnosis.","C":"CEA is not a diagnostic gatekeeper for colonoscopy.","D":"Bright-red blood does not establish a benign cause.","E":"Correct. Investigate the symptom through the appropriate diagnostic pathway."},"key_takeaways":["Recognise that symptomatic patients should not be managed solely through population screening."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Australian Government","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/doing-a-bowel-screening-test/who-should-do-the-bowel-screening-test-and-who-should-not","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"27e10742-11d5-56c5-8e38-915074e4bf6b","B":"79ebcc11-6da9-553b-a177-8b9b2d3c30f2","C":"a3fca79e-5087-5cb7-987e-64d82abab27f","D":"117c4a98-9606-58a6-80b3-fb56078dfbb2","E":"edec84cc-84a7-540c-825d-63a1d6675f2e"},"correct_option_id":"edec84cc-84a7-540c-825d-63a1d6675f2e","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-10","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,1,1.0,'tier=4 (ph01)'),
('a171f448-03af-5737-b9f3-1a380b579543'::uuid,'ZQ-0411','population-health-ethics','bowel-screening','A 58-year-old woman in Wollongong had a complete colonoscopy 14 months ago, during which a 6 mm tubular adenoma was removed. Her specialist documented a five-year surveillance interval. She has no bowel symptoms. A program screening kit arrives, and she asks whether she should complete it as well.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Do not complete the program kit now; follow the individualised colonoscopy surveillance plan.","Complete the kit because a government invitation overrides the specialist plan.","Replace the planned surveillance colonoscopy with biennial kits.","Order CEA and cancel all further bowel surveillance if it is normal.","Start annual kits because an adenoma was previously found."]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER
A. Do not complete the program kit now; follow the individualised colonoscopy surveillance plan.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
A recent colonoscopy and personal history of adenoma may make routine population screening inappropriate at that time. The patient should follow the documented surveillance plan and clarify any uncertainty with the treating clinician.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Correct. A recent colonoscopy and planned surveillance take precedence over automatically repeating population screening.
B. Invitations do not override individual clinical circumstances.
C. A screening kit does not replace indicated colonoscopic surveillance.
D. A normal CEA cannot exclude colorectal cancer.
E. A previous adenoma does not automatically mandate annual FIT.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Reconcile population screening invitations with an existing individual surveillance plan.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Reconcile population screening invitations with an existing individual surveillance plan.

EVIDENCE
