('e135b921-646b-5158-963f-ac5e40d330e8'::uuid,'ZQ-0412','population-health-ethics','bowel-screening','A 78-year-old man in Launceston is independent and has no bowel symptoms. His younger brother has just completed a mailed screening kit. His son reads that the national program covers people up to age 80 and asks the GP to order another kit for his father.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Request an automatic program kit because he is fit and well.","Explain that he is outside the program''s routine age range; discuss individual options if appropriate.","Arrange urgent colonoscopy because no kit has arrived.","Tell him that no bowel investigation is appropriate after age 74, even if symptoms arise.","Order annual CEA instead of a screening kit."]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER
B. Explain that he is outside the program''s routine age range; discuss individual options if appropriate.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
The National Bowel Cancer Screening Program covers eligible people aged 45–74. Above 74, there is no routine program invitation; any further screening decision should consider benefits, harms, health status and preferences. New symptoms still require assessment.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Fitness alone does not extend routine program eligibility.
B. Correct. Discuss individual options rather than assuming automatic eligibility.
C. Absence of a kit is not itself an indication for colonoscopy.
D. Age does not remove the need to investigate future symptoms.
E. CEA is not an alternative screening program.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply the upper age limit while avoiding the error of withholding symptom-based care.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply the upper age limit while avoiding the error of withholding symptom-based care.

