('564a7c2d-acc3-5681-b5ee-1f7fa0584c0d'::uuid,'ZQ-0414','population-health-ethics','cervical-screening','A 28-year-old woman in Brisbane completed HPV vaccination at school. She has had sexual contact, has no symptoms and has never had a Cervical Screening Test. A pharmacy clinic tells her that vaccination protects her for life and she can ignore screening invitations.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Arrange annual Pap smears because vaccination changes the interval.","Defer screening until age 30 because she was vaccinated.","Arrange colposcopy as the first screening test.","She should have a Cervical Screening Test now.","Cancel all future screening because vaccination is sufficient."]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER
D. She should have a Cervical Screening Test now.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
An eligible 28-year-old who has ever been sexually active should participate in the National Cervical Screening Program. HPV vaccination reduces the risk of infection from covered types but does not eliminate all oncogenic HPV risk.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Annual Pap smears are not the routine current pathway.
B. There is no need to delay until age 30.
C. Colposcopy is not the routine first-line screening test for an asymptomatic person.
D. Correct. Arrange screening under the current program.
E. Vaccination does not replace screening.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Explain the continuing role of HPV-based screening after vaccination.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Explain the continuing role of HPV-based screening after vaccination.

EVIDENCE
