('f6eca0cb-07fe-5417-98eb-d998b0f598de'::uuid,'ZQ-0415','population-health-ethics','cervical-screening','A 36-year-old woman in Adelaide had a Cervical Screening Test four years ago. The oncogenic HPV test was negative. She has no symptoms. Her practice recall system, still configured for two-yearly Pap smears, labels her overdue.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Repeat the test today because every cervical screen is due after two years.","Arrange annual cytology until she catches up with the old schedule.","Refer for colposcopy because four years have passed.","Discharge her permanently because she has one negative result.","Explain that routine screening is generally five-yearly after a negative HPV test, so she is not yet due."]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER
E. Explain that routine screening is generally five-yearly after a negative HPV test, so she is not yet due.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
The Australian program uses HPV-based testing at five-year intervals for eligible participants with a negative result. A legacy recall based on the former two-year Pap schedule should not override current guidance.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. This applies an outdated interval.
B. Annual cytology is not the standard routine pathway.
C. A prior negative HPV result alone does not warrant colposcopy.
D. A negative result does not mean screening can stop permanently.
E. Correct. The next routine test is due at five years, assuming no new symptoms or other indication.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply the five-year interval after a negative HPV-based screening result.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply the five-year interval after a negative HPV-based screening result.

EVIDENCE
