('88c6a66c-7579-576a-a483-004c5eeca029'::uuid,'ZQ-0416','population-health-ethics','cervical-screening','A 41-year-old woman in Perth has a positive cervical screening test for HPV 16. Reflex liquid-based cytology shows no intraepithelial lesion. She has no bleeding or other symptoms. A colleague suggests repeating the test in 12 months because the cytology is normal.

What is the most appropriate next step?','["Refer for colposcopic assessment.","Repeat HPV testing in five years.","Repeat HPV testing in 12 months without referral.","Repeat cytology alone in six weeks.","Discharge her from the program because cytology is negative."]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER
A. Refer for colposcopic assessment.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Under the Australian cervical screening pathway, detection of HPV 16 or 18 warrants referral for colposcopic assessment regardless of the reflex cytology result. Normal cytology does not negate the significance of high-risk HPV 16.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Correct. Arrange colposcopy.
B. Five-year routine recall is inappropriate after HPV 16 detection.
C. The 12-month repeat pathway for some non-16/18 HPV results does not apply here.
D. Cytology alone does not adequately manage the result.
E. A normal cytology result does not cancel the high-risk HPV finding.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Interpret HPV 16/18 results within the Australian cervical screening pathway.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Interpret HPV 16/18 results within the Australian cervical screening pathway.

EVIDENCE
