('8b807886-1111-5db7-b446-896f9f5b538d'::uuid,'ZQ-0417','population-health-ethics','cervical-screening','A 33-year-old woman in Hobart has non-16/18 oncogenic HPV detected on cervical screening, with low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (LSIL) on reflex cytology. She has no symptoms. A registrar proposes cone biopsy so the issue is resolved in one procedure.

What is the most appropriate next step?','["Refer directly for cone biopsy as the routine next step.","Repeat the Cervical Screening Test in 12 months, following the recommended pathway.","Discharge her because the HPV type is not 16 or 18.","Repeat cytology alone in five years.","Arrange routine five-year screening without follow-up."]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER
B. Repeat the Cervical Screening Test in 12 months, following the recommended pathway.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
For non-16/18 oncogenic HPV with negative or low-grade cytology, the Australian pathway generally recommends repeat HPV-based testing in 12 months. Persistent HPV or a higher-risk result can trigger further assessment.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Cone biopsy is not the routine immediate step for this result.
B. Correct. Arrange the recommended 12-month follow-up.
C. Non-16/18 oncogenic HPV still requires appropriate follow-up.
D. Cytology alone is not the recommended surveillance approach.
E. Five-year routine recall is inappropriate before the recommended follow-up.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Manage non-16/18 HPV with low-grade cytology without over-treating or losing follow-up.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Manage non-16/18 HPV with low-grade cytology without over-treating or losing follow-up.

EVIDENCE
National Cervical Screening Program: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program','moderate','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0417","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-17","group_population_health_ethics","sys_cervical_screening","task_management","level_3","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","HPV_non16_18","low_grade_abnormality","follow_up_interval","overtreatment"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"National Cervical Screening Program\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Cone biopsy is not the routine immediate step for this result.","B":"Correct. Arrange the recommended 12-month follow-up.","C":"Non-16/18 oncogenic HPV still requires appropriate follow-up.","D":"Cytology alone is not the recommended surveillance approach.","E":"Five-year routine recall is inappropriate before the recommended follow-up."},"key_takeaways":["Manage non-16/18 HPV with low-grade cytology without over-treating or losing follow-up."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"National Cervical Screening Program","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"2fe8e4c7-8111-5387-b555-134e85529881","B":"70a3f62d-1374-5eef-a799-2deb7017cd8d","C":"921c6dec-0227-5d96-8a05-b6fde35bf858","D":"d1de4417-f305-5993-964f-0ef3c0678258","E":"462ae22b-5b86-5250-92e4-9cd0403973d4"},"correct_option_id":"70a3f62d-1374-5eef-a799-2deb7017cd8d","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-17","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,0,1.0,'tier=3 (ph01)'),
('cb790fa9-ee35-5de8-888d-28b8a42f066e'::uuid,'ZQ-0418','population-health-ethics','cervical-screening','A 29-year-old woman in Cairns reports six weeks of postcoital bleeding. She has had sexual contact, has never been screened and received HPV vaccination at school. Speculum examination is incomplete. Her GP suggests taking a routine screening sample and offering five-year recall if HPV is not detected.

What is the most appropriate next step?','["Reassure her because cervical cancer is uncommon under 30.","Offer an HPV vaccine dose instead of investigation.","Arrange diagnostic assessment with appropriate cervical examination and clinician-collected co-testing; refer for specialist assessment and colposcopy if bleeding persists or recurs.","Take a routine screening test and provide five-year recall if negative.","Arrange a self-collected sample in 12 months without further assessment."]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER
C. Assess the bleeding diagnostically, including appropriate cervical examination and testing, with referral if indicated.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Postcoital bleeding is a symptom, not a screening indication. Arrange appropriate diagnostic assessment, including a clinician-collected cervical co-test where indicated and completion of the speculum examination or referral if it cannot be completed. Persistent or recurrent bleeding requires specialist gynaecological assessment, with colposcopy as indicated. A negative screening result must not close the diagnostic work-up.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Age and vaccination do not justify dismissing persistent bleeding.
B. Vaccination is preventive, not a diagnostic intervention.
C. Correct. Complete or arrange appropriate diagnostic examination and clinician-collected co-testing; persistent or recurrent bleeding warrants specialist gynaecological assessment and colposcopy as indicated.
D. A routine negative screen does not exclude all causes of symptomatic bleeding.
E. Delaying assessment for 12 months is inappropriate.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Distinguish symptomatic cervical assessment from routine population screening.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Distinguish symptomatic cervical assessment from routine population screening.

EVIDENCE
National Cervical Screening Program: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program','difficult','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0418","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-18","group_population_health_ethics","sys_cervical_screening","task_management","level_4","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","symptom_based_investigation","cervical_red_flags","screening_vs_diagnosis","diagnostic_delay"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"National Cervical Screening Program\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Age and vaccination do not justify dismissing persistent bleeding.","B":"Vaccination is preventive, not a diagnostic intervention.","C":"Correct. Investigate the symptom and refer where the findings warrant it.","D":"A routine negative screen does not exclude all causes of symptomatic bleeding.","E":"Delaying assessment for 12 months is inappropriate."},"key_takeaways":["Distinguish symptomatic cervical assessment from routine population screening."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"National Cervical Screening Program","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"e3139699-84e5-5b93-a52f-224100479ee6","B":"aa676187-0afc-5487-92ef-a93f1a8dddc2","C":"8cbaf149-6319-56c8-855b-5a800f7b6fbb","D":"f3fd7858-45f6-5569-af99-ea98cdda57fd","E":"71b8ab17-438c-5aae-ab51-8762894b4819"},"correct_option_id":"8cbaf149-6319-56c8-855b-5a800f7b6fbb","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-18","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,1,1.0,'tier=4 (ph01)'),
('76571295-c824-550e-b072-349bb17c22a0'::uuid,'ZQ-0419','population-health-ethics','cervical-screening','A 48-year-old woman in Alice Springs is due for cervical screening. Her previous HPV test was negative five years ago. She has no symptoms but declines a speculum examination after a difficult previous experience. The receptionist says self-collection is only available to women who are years overdue.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Explain that she must have a clinician-collected sample or no screening.","Refer for colposcopy instead of screening.","Tell her to return only if symptoms develop.","Explain that eligible participants can choose a self-collected vaginal HPV sample.","Repeat a Pap smear every two years until she accepts self-collection."]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER
D. Explain that eligible participants can choose a self-collected vaginal HPV sample.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
The Australian National Cervical Screening Program permits eligible participants to choose self-collection or clinician collection for HPV testing. Self-collection is not restricted solely to people who are overdue. A positive result may require additional clinician-collected testing or further assessment depending on the result and pathway.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Incorrect. Self-collection is an available option for eligible participants.
B. Colposcopy is not the routine alternative to a screening sample.
C. Screening is still recommended even when the patient has no symptoms.
D. Correct. Discuss self-collection and explain the follow-up pathway.
E. This relies on an outdated Pap-smear interval.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Use self-collection to improve access to cervical screening.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Use self-collection to improve access to cervical screening.

EVIDENCE
National Cervical Screening Program: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program','moderate','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0419","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-19","group_population_health_ethics","sys_cervical_screening","task_management","level_3","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","self_collection","screening_access","patient_preference","barrier_reduction"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"National Cervical Screening Program\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Incorrect. Self-collection is an available option for eligible participants.","B":"Colposcopy is not the routine alternative to a screening sample.","C":"Screening is still recommended even when the patient has no symptoms.","D":"Correct. Discuss self-collection and explain the follow-up pathway.","E":"This relies on an outdated Pap-smear interval."},"key_takeaways":["Use self-collection to improve access to cervical screening."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"National Cervical Screening Program","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-cervical-screening-program","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"8918e6bf-6f3c-5d5b-9155-667feff9c95b","B":"58f05145-b236-5ff4-8fbe-eb4b96c8d741","C":"fc1c98d4-e8cc-5d84-9e7d-49d717aae2b3","D":"903539e5-01ac-5b82-b4af-89c47c6a280b","E":"31b6c4a0-5773-5e2f-aacc-45429b6c3896"},"correct_option_id":"903539e5-01ac-5b82-b4af-89c47c6a280b","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-19","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,0,1.0,'tier=3 (ph01)'),
('44d35a96-29f3-5e1c-a6a0-9feebe2194e3'::uuid,'ZQ-0420','population-health-ethics','cervical-screening','A 60-year-old woman in Bendigo had a total hysterectomy six years ago for fibroids. The cervix was removed, and histology showed no cervical intraepithelial neoplasia. She has no vaginal bleeding. A screening reminder arrives, and she asks whether she needs vaginal vault smears until age 74.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Arrange five-yearly vault screening because every woman remains eligible until 74.","Arrange annual vaginal cytology because her cervix has been removed.","Refer for colposcopy because a reminder indicates high risk.","Arrange self-collected HPV testing every two years.","She can cease routine cervical screening because she has no cervix and no relevant history of cervical precancer."]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER
E. She can cease routine cervical screening because she has no cervix and no relevant history of cervical precancer.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
A person who has had a total hysterectomy with removal of the cervix for benign disease, with no history of high-grade cervical precancer or cervical cancer, generally does not need routine cervical screening. Symptoms such as vaginal bleeding still require clinical assessment.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. The usual screening age range does not override the absence of a cervix and lack of relevant prior disease.
B. Routine vault cytology is not indicated in this scenario.
C. An automated reminder is not evidence of high risk.
D. Self-collection is not a substitute for unnecessary vault screening.
E. Correct. Confirm the operative and histology records and update the recall system.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply screening exit criteria after total hysterectomy.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply screening exit criteria after total hysterectomy.

EVIDENCE
