('69868bfa-fc51-5872-81bf-ebcedf70d610'::uuid,'ZQ-0421','population-health-ethics','lung-screening','A 61-year-old man in Melbourne has smoked 20 cigarettes daily since age 21 and continues to smoke. He has no persistent change in cough, haemoptysis, weight loss or unexplained breathlessness. His brother had annual chest X-rays decades ago. He is willing to quit but has not set a date.

What is the most appropriate screening advice?','["Assess eligibility for the National Lung Cancer Screening Program and offer low-dose CT if he meets the criteria.","Arrange annual chest X-rays.","Collect yearly sputum cytology.","Delay screening until he has stopped smoking for four weeks.","Wait until age 65 before assessing eligibility."]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER
A. Assess eligibility for the National Lung Cancer Screening Program and offer low-dose CT if he meets the criteria.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Australia''s National Lung Cancer Screening Program launched on 1 July 2025. Eligibility includes age 50–70, no symptoms suggesting lung cancer, current smoking or cessation within the past 10 years, and at least 30 pack-years of cigarette smoking. This patient has approximately 40 pack-years and appears to meet the core criteria. A healthcare provider must assess and confirm eligibility.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Correct. Assess the criteria and arrange program entry if eligible.
B. Chest X-ray is not the program screening test.
C. Sputum cytology is not the entry screening test.
D. Smoking cessation is strongly encouraged, but quitting is not a prerequisite for participation.
E. He is already within the program''s age range.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply the current Australian lung screening eligibility criteria.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply the current Australian lung screening eligibility criteria.

EVIDENCE
Australian Government: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works','moderate','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0421","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-21","group_population_health_ethics","sys_lung_screening","task_management","level_3","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","lung_screening_eligibility","pack_year_calculation","current_guidelines","smoking_cessation"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Australian Government\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Correct. Assess the criteria and arrange program entry if eligible.","B":"Chest X-ray is not the program screening test.","C":"Sputum cytology is not the entry screening test.","D":"Smoking cessation is strongly encouraged, but quitting is not a prerequisite for participation.","E":"He is already within the program''s age range."},"key_takeaways":["Apply the current Australian lung screening eligibility criteria."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Australian Government","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"6c9badfb-d17d-593d-a340-e27cbcfac3ea","B":"304f1ea4-4836-5503-a8ee-22f09e0d1190","C":"337c14d7-4772-5011-a07f-28fcf321e117","D":"6a512a32-e163-5cf2-9c04-6b7090450878","E":"5ed6f9ff-7c8a-5e26-b2e9-68335057c315"},"correct_option_id":"6c9badfb-d17d-593d-a340-e27cbcfac3ea","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-21","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,0,1.0,'tier=3 (ph01)'),
('b16e52b8-0a76-5741-835f-75e0cfbd2374'::uuid,'ZQ-0422','population-health-ethics','lung-screening','A 55-year-old woman in Townsville quit smoking 12 years ago after smoking one pack daily for 35 years. She has no symptoms and has never enrolled in the National Lung Cancer Screening Program. Her GP calculates 35 pack-years and prepares to refer her for free low-dose CT screening.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Refer her because the pack-year threshold is sufficient on its own.","Explain that she does not meet initial-entry criteria because she quit 12 years ago; any continuation rule is relevant only to people already enrolled.","Arrange annual chest X-rays as an alternative screening program.","Use sputum cytology instead of low-dose CT.","Refer her for screening PET-CT because she is outside the program criteria."]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER
B. Explain that she does not meet the program''s current smoking-cessation time criterion for entry.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
For initial entry, the current program requires eligible participants to be aged 50–70, asymptomatic, have at least 30 pack-years and currently smoke or have quit within the previous 10 years. She has 35 pack-years but quit 12 years ago and has never enrolled, so she does not meet the initial-entry criterion. Any separate continuation rule must not be applied to a person who is seeking initial entry.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Pack-years alone are insufficient.
B. Correct. She is seeking initial entry and quit 12 years ago. Do not apply a continuation rule for people already enrolled to a new entrant.
C. Annual chest X-ray is not an equivalent recommended screening substitute.
D. Sputum cytology is not an alternative population screening pathway.
E. PET-CT is not a routine population screening substitute.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply all eligibility criteria rather than making decisions from smoking exposure alone.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply all eligibility criteria rather than making decisions from smoking exposure alone.

EVIDENCE
Australian Government: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works','difficult','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0422","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-22","group_population_health_ethics","sys_lung_screening","task_management","level_4","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","eligibility_boundary","pack_years","quit_date","inappropriate_imaging"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Australian Government\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Pack-years alone are insufficient.","B":"Correct. Explain the eligibility criteria and offer appropriate preventive care.","C":"Annual chest X-ray is not an equivalent recommended screening substitute.","D":"Sputum cytology is not an alternative population screening pathway.","E":"PET-CT is not a routine population screening substitute."},"key_takeaways":["Apply all eligibility criteria rather than making decisions from smoking exposure alone."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Australian Government","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"4f2c42a9-9128-5ba2-a5de-fa48ac3a2484","B":"fa603315-de46-5e82-99c1-6516d70315f8","C":"3ef106b5-74f0-59fe-ab5f-a5982ea84ffe","D":"10d195b7-b71c-5ed6-8254-270377c8af30","E":"93ea29da-f9a3-5bd3-a14f-14489f0037de"},"correct_option_id":"fa603315-de46-5e82-99c1-6516d70315f8","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-22","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,1,1.0,'tier=4 (ph01)'),
('cfedf165-254c-5ab9-bb16-f8f0338d9e5d'::uuid,'ZQ-0423','population-health-ethics','lung-screening','A 64-year-old man in Newcastle has a 40 pack-year smoking history and continues to smoke. For 10 days, he has noticed small streaks of blood when coughing. He has no weight loss and brings a lung screening form to request a free low-dose CT.

What is the most appropriate next step?','["Complete the routine screening request and review the CT in two years.","Arrange a screening chest X-ray before considering CT.","Investigate the haemoptysis through an appropriate diagnostic pathway rather than enrolling him as an asymptomatic screening participant.","Ask him to stop smoking for four weeks before arranging imaging.","Order sputum cytology and proceed with screening only if it is abnormal."]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER
C. Investigate the haemoptysis through an appropriate diagnostic pathway rather than enrolling him as an asymptomatic screening participant.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
The National Lung Cancer Screening Program is intended for asymptomatic people. Haemoptysis is a potentially significant symptom requiring prompt clinical assessment and appropriate diagnostic investigation. Screening eligibility does not override a symptomatic presentation.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. The screening pathway is inappropriate while a potentially concerning symptom is present.
B. A screening chest X-ray does not adequately address the diagnostic question.
C. Correct. Investigate the symptom, with urgency determined by clinical assessment.
D. Imaging should not be delayed for smoking cessation.
E. Sputum cytology is not an appropriate substitute for diagnostic assessment.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Separate screening eligibility from investigation of suspected disease.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Separate screening eligibility from investigation of suspected disease.

EVIDENCE
