('66a6e14e-61b1-5922-8572-c50b7aede749'::uuid,'ZQ-0424','population-health-ethics','lung-screening','A 52-year-old woman in Fremantle smokes 10 cigarettes daily and has done so since age 32. She has no respiratory symptoms. A poster says the national lung screening program is available for people aged 50–70 who smoke. She asks her GP to arrange the free scan.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["She qualifies because she is within the age range and currently smokes.","She should have annual chest X-rays until her smoking exposure increases.","She can request a screening PET scan instead.","She does not meet the minimum 30 pack-year threshold for program entry.","She must stop smoking before any screening assessment can occur."]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER
D. She does not meet the minimum 30 pack-year threshold for program entry.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Ten cigarettes daily is half a pack per day. Over 20 years, this gives approximately 10 pack-years, below the program''s 30 pack-year minimum.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Age and current smoking are necessary but not sufficient criteria.
B. Annual chest X-ray is not the recommended population screening alternative.
C. PET scanning is not a screening substitute.
D. Correct. Explain the pack-year calculation and support smoking cessation.
E. She does not need to quit before discussing eligibility, although quitting should be encouraged.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Calculate pack-years and apply the complete eligibility criteria.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Calculate pack-years and apply the complete eligibility criteria.

EVIDENCE
Australian Government: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works','moderate','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0424","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-24","group_population_health_ethics","sys_lung_screening","task_management","level_3","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","pack_year_calculation","threshold_application","lung_screening","smoking_cessation"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Australian Government\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Age and current smoking are necessary but not sufficient criteria.","B":"Annual chest X-ray is not the recommended population screening alternative.","C":"PET scanning is not a screening substitute.","D":"Correct. Explain the pack-year calculation and support smoking cessation.","E":"She does not need to quit before discussing eligibility, although quitting should be encouraged."},"key_takeaways":["Calculate pack-years and apply the complete eligibility criteria."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Australian Government","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"a3a6d4b2-a235-5b78-9ed2-9fde6abea502","B":"e800f646-4315-5bf6-a46d-eae4fe0c5982","C":"47c35ca4-5c51-5433-9b51-3470359b10ae","D":"17b973ef-17d4-51c0-af03-77dfbbecbd16","E":"ed13531f-9ee4-5e36-b2b2-304cfd4a6cac"},"correct_option_id":"17b973ef-17d4-51c0-af03-77dfbbecbd16","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-24","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,0,1.0,'tier=3 (ph01)'),
('f9348353-202d-540b-86f9-68aaf31b2013'::uuid,'ZQ-0425','population-health-ethics','other-cancer-screening','A 58-year-old man in Sydney has no urinary symptoms and no family history of prostate cancer. His employer offers an annual health panel including PSA. He asks to join a national prostate screening program with automatic recalls similar to the bowel screening program.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Enrol him in a national biennial PSA program.","Arrange annual PSA testing because all men over 50 require it.","Arrange prostate MRI every two years as the standard screening test.","Explain that a normal PSA result would eliminate the need for future discussion.","Explain that Australia has no organised population-based prostate cancer screening program and discuss PSA testing individually if he is interested."]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER
E. Explain that Australia has no organised population-based prostate cancer screening program and discuss PSA testing individually if he is interested.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Australia does not have an organised population-based prostate cancer screening program. PSA testing in asymptomatic men involves weighing potential benefits against false positives, overdiagnosis and treatment-related harms. An informed individual decision is appropriate.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. No national program provides automatic biennial PSA screening.
B. PSA testing is not universally mandatory for all men over 50.
C. MRI is not an organised population screening test.
D. A normal PSA does not eliminate future risk or future clinical decisions.
E. Correct. Explain the absence of a population program and discuss individual testing preferences.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Distinguish individual early-detection testing from an organised population screening program.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Distinguish individual early-detection testing from an organised population screening program.

EVIDENCE
Cancer Council Australia: https://www.cancer.org.au/cancer-information/screening-tests-and-treatments/early-detection-and-screening/early-detection-of-prostate-cancer','easy','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0425","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-25","group_population_health_ethics","sys_other_cancer_screening","task_management","level_2","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","screening_program_scope","PSA","informed_decision","overdiagnosis"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Cancer Council Australia\",\"url\":\"https://www.cancer.org.au/cancer-information/screening-tests-and-treatments/early-detection-and-screening/early-detection-of-prostate-cancer\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"No national program provides automatic biennial PSA screening.","B":"PSA testing is not universally mandatory for all men over 50.","C":"MRI is not an organised population screening test.","D":"A normal PSA does not eliminate future risk or future clinical decisions.","E":"Correct. Explain the absence of a population program and discuss individual testing preferences."},"key_takeaways":["Distinguish individual early-detection testing from an organised population screening program."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Cancer Council Australia","url":"https://www.cancer.org.au/cancer-information/screening-tests-and-treatments/early-detection-and-screening/early-detection-of-prostate-cancer","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"1960c3d7-3bd6-52f6-a230-3d9449a74e86","B":"dd22dac1-064d-5b22-8e9d-107e1140251f","C":"63b35110-bb9f-5c55-8ddf-1015ce4c885b","D":"320ca462-d918-56ef-b16e-d0981217e13a","E":"1da47d14-2574-5219-96e7-a18ba71f5ad2"},"correct_option_id":"1da47d14-2574-5219-96e7-a18ba71f5ad2","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-25","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,-1,1.0,'tier=2 (ph01)'),
('4546477d-950c-57b2-9600-320bd9fd70f2'::uuid,'ZQ-0426','population-health-ethics','other-cancer-screening','A 45-year-old woman in Brisbane has no changing mole or other skin symptoms. A private skin-check clinic offers her enrolment in a supposed national melanoma recall program, with whole-body photography every two years. She asks whether this is a standard Australian screening service.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Explain that Australia has no national population-based skin cancer screening program; discuss individual risk and skin assessment.","Enrol her in a national two-yearly whole-body photography program.","Arrange annual biopsies of two normal moles.","Explain that one normal skin examination eliminates future risk.","Arrange a screening PET scan."]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER
A. Explain that Australia has no national population-based skin cancer screening program; discuss individual risk and skin assessment.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Australia does not have a national population-based skin cancer screening program. Individual skin assessment may be appropriate according to personal risk, lesion characteristics and clinical advice. New or changing lesions should be assessed diagnostically.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Correct. Clarify the difference between individual assessment and a national program.
B. There is no national recall program of this kind.
C. Routine biopsy of normal moles is not appropriate.
D. A normal examination does not eliminate future risk.
E. PET is not a population screening test for melanoma.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Recognise the limits of national screening programs while promoting appropriate skin awareness and assessment.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Recognise the limits of national screening programs while promoting appropriate skin awareness and assessment.

EVIDENCE
Australian Government: https://www.health.gov.au/topics/cancer/screening-for-cancer','easy','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0426","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-26","group_population_health_ethics","sys_other_cancer_screening","task_management","level_2","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","screening_program_scope","melanoma","overinvestigation","patient_education"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Australian Government\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/topics/cancer/screening-for-cancer\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Correct. Clarify the difference between individual assessment and a national program.","B":"There is no national recall program of this kind.","C":"Routine biopsy of normal moles is not appropriate.","D":"A normal examination does not eliminate future risk.","E":"PET is not a population screening test for melanoma."},"key_takeaways":["Recognise the limits of national screening programs while promoting appropriate skin awareness and assessment."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Australian Government","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/topics/cancer/screening-for-cancer","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"d00dd4d4-a7e9-56df-8503-51d6f40a0664","B":"d42885b6-f45d-5557-be27-9c0c958345c0","C":"884a3ba5-ba9f-5fe1-85d8-8c98025c6db1","D":"a8b780b6-1ef9-5997-849f-0537800ded68","E":"72264baa-be4a-5bf6-a581-e25a31c3450c"},"correct_option_id":"d00dd4d4-a7e9-56df-8503-51d6f40a0664","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-26","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,-1,1.0,'tier=2 (ph01)'),
('afd66d66-afd4-5e86-9761-9d87b47345bb'::uuid,'ZQ-0427','population-health-ethics','other-cancer-screening','A 54-year-old woman in Canberra has no pelvic symptoms. Her mother developed ovarian cancer at 72, and there are no other known affected relatives. A wellness clinic offers annual CA-125 blood tests and pelvic ultrasound as an ovarian equivalent of BreastScreen. Examination is normal.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Enrol her in annual CA-125 testing and pelvic ultrasound.","Explain that routine population ovarian screening is not recommended; take a full family pedigree and assess whether familial cancer risk referral is warranted.","Use CA-125 every six months instead of ultrasound.","Explain that a normal CA-125 result eliminates her familial risk.","Prescribe a combined oral contraceptive as the screening test."]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER
B. Explain that routine population ovarian screening is not recommended, while assessing whether her family history warrants hereditary cancer risk assessment.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
CA-125 and transvaginal ultrasound have not established an effective routine population screening strategy for asymptomatic women. A single first-degree relative diagnosed at age 72 does not automatically establish a high-risk familial category. Take a complete pedigree, including cancer types and ages at diagnosis, and use the full history to determine whether specialist familial cancer assessment is warranted.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Routine annual CA-125 and ultrasound are not recommended population screening.
B. Correct. Avoid ineffective routine screening, obtain a full pedigree, and refer for familial risk assessment only if the overall history meets the relevant criteria.
C. Increasing the frequency of CA-125 does not solve the limitations of screening.
D. A normal CA-125 cannot exclude ovarian cancer or hereditary susceptibility.
E. Oral contraception is not a screening test.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Distinguish the absence of an effective population screening program from the need to assess inherited cancer risk.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Distinguish the absence of an effective population screening program from the need to assess inherited cancer risk.

EVIDENCE
Australian Government: https://www.health.gov.au/topics/cancer/screening-for-cancer','difficult','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0427","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-27","group_population_health_ethics","sys_other_cancer_screening","task_management","level_4","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","ovarian_screening","familial_risk","tumour_marker_limitations","genetic_referral"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Australian Government\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/topics/cancer/screening-for-cancer\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Routine annual CA-125 and ultrasound are not recommended population screening.","B":"Correct. Avoid ineffective routine screening and assess inherited risk appropriately.","C":"Increasing the frequency of CA-125 does not solve the limitations of screening.","D":"A normal CA-125 cannot exclude ovarian cancer or hereditary susceptibility.","E":"Oral contraception is not a screening test."},"key_takeaways":["Distinguish the absence of an effective population screening program from the need to assess inherited cancer risk."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Australian Government","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/topics/cancer/screening-for-cancer","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"5856e38f-953e-5662-8840-e0e95eaabd76","B":"09aa20b9-f69e-5524-9085-e70fb56f7150","C":"0dda58b8-29ba-5501-a312-58027eed5c52","D":"0e17bdf9-bf93-56a9-9151-03cc98866d88","E":"b08175fe-14d2-54b2-88c4-129196cbd1ab"},"correct_option_id":"09aa20b9-f69e-5524-9085-e70fb56f7150","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-27","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,1,1.0,'tier=4 (ph01)'),
('457efacc-f985-5e34-a34a-053caa86ba0b'::uuid,'ZQ-0428','population-health-ethics','bowel-screening','A 49-year-old woman in Mildura has no bowel symptoms and no known family history of colorectal cancer. She has never participated in screening. Her 51-year-old neighbour received a National Bowel Cancer Screening Program kit automatically last month. She asks whether she should wait for her own kit to arrive.

What is the most appropriate advice?','["Explain that automatic invitations begin at 45 and she should wait for the mail-out.","Explain that she cannot participate until age 50.","Explain that she can request her first free kit now; automatic mail-outs generally begin at age 50.","Arrange colonoscopy as the first test for every person aged 49.","Advise her to wait until symptoms develop before screening."]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER
C. Explain that she can request her first free kit now; automatic mail-outs generally begin at age 50.

CLINICAL EXPLANATION
Eligible Australians aged 45–49 can request their first free bowel screening kit. Automatic mail-outs are generally provided every two years to eligible people aged 50–74. A 49-year-old does not need to wait until age 50 to begin participating.

OPTION-BY-OPTION ANALYSIS
A. Automatic mail-outs do not routinely start at 45.
B. Eligibility begins at 45, not 50.
C. Correct. Explain how she can request her first kit.
D. Colonoscopy is not the default first test for every asymptomatic 49-year-old.
E. Screening is intended to detect disease before symptoms develop.

LEARNING OBJECTIVE
Apply the current age-based access and invitation rules of the National Bowel Cancer Screening Program.

KEY TAKEAWAY
Apply the current age-based access and invitation rules of the National Bowel Cancer Screening Program.

EVIDENCE
Australian Government: https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/doing-a-bowel-screening-test/who-should-do-the-bowel-screening-test-and-who-should-not','moderate','{"source":"qns_v2_ph01","source_batch":"ph01","review_status":"draft_pending_independent_clinical_signoff","tags":["ZQ-0428","zyntra_bank","qns_v2","ph01_screening","PH-S-28","group_population_health_ethics","sys_bowel_screening","task_management","level_3","clinical_review_pending_2026_10_10","bowel_screening_eligibility","program_access","age_threshold","preventive_care"],"clinician_task":"management","guideline_reference":"{\"name\":\"Australian Government\",\"url\":\"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/doing-a-bowel-screening-test/who-should-do-the-bowel-screening-test-and-who-should-not\"}","incorrect_answer_explanations":{"A":"Automatic mail-outs do not routinely start at 45.","B":"Eligibility begins at 45, not 50.","C":"Correct. Explain how she can request her first kit.","D":"Colonoscopy is not the default first test for every asymptomatic 49-year-old.","E":"Screening is intended to detect disease before symptoms develop."},"key_takeaways":["Apply the current age-based access and invitation rules of the National Bowel Cancer Screening Program."],"evidence_sources":[{"name":"Australian Government","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/doing-a-bowel-screening-test/who-should-do-the-bowel-screening-test-and-who-should-not","checked_on":"2026-10-10"}],"option_ids":{"A":"c3ced98e-88b9-5a1b-b15c-ef34d2af2e73","B":"f2fb5af7-5a67-5db2-bd76-b71d5eaf1374","C":"4b5cda47-2f04-51cf-841f-9ea95fb8ae61","D":"dd5a549d-6522-54fc-98e1-70b13065b118","E":"09ae1be6-9294-56f6-ae0a-7b358aa1dac0"},"correct_option_id":"4b5cda47-2f04-51cf-841f-9ea95fb8ae61","question_version":1,"phase_id":"PH-01","source_question_id":"PH-S-28","reviewer_attribution_requested":"Dr. GopalaKrishnan SankaraNarayanan","review_date":"2026-10-10","lifecycle_status":"draft","imported_by":"0081"}'::jsonb,0,1.0,'tier=3 (ph01)')
